Note the whole method is @Transactional, so if we return view(batch, order) here, the tryAddReceived update we already did would COMMIT — that's wrong! We already incremented collected via tryAddReceived before registering the serial. If the serial was concurrently registered by another single receipt, our tryAddReceived may have succeeded (different version? no — both would use same expectedVersion presumably... actually the concurrent request could carry a different expectedVersion).

Order of operations problem: tryAddReceived happens BEFORE serial registration. If serial registration then fails and we "replay" (return view), our collected increment commits → double collection! Must throw instead to roll back, OR register serial BEFORE tryAddReceived.

Better order: register bank serial BEFORE tryAddReceived (after validations). Then if registration fails → replay/409 decision before any mutation:
- If MASTER → throw 409 (rollback, nothing changed anyway).
- If SINGLE with same amount → return view (no mutation done yet — safe).
- If SINGLE with different amount → throw 409.

Then tryAddReceived, then insert receipt. If receipt insert hits unique violation (shouldn't happen since we hold the serial registration... but a legacy path? no) — fine.

But wait: registering serial before tryAddReceived means if tryAddReceived later fails (version conflict), the tx rolls back and the serial registration rolls back too. Good — "失败不占键" semantics preserved.

So new order in registerReceipt:
1. load batch/order/detail, APPROVED check.
2. sameSerial receipt check (replay/409) — existing.
3. master-kind check → 409.
4. COMPLETED check, version check, over-amount check — existing.
5. tryRegister SINGLE; if fail → resolve as above (replay/409), no mutation yet.
6. tryAddReceived — existing (0 → re-read & throw).
7. insert receipt.

Hmm, but step 5's replay path returns view(batch, order) — with no mutations, safe to commit.

One more consideration: two concurrent single receipts with the SAME serial and SAME amount and same version: both pass step 2 (no receipt yet), both pass validations, one wins tryRegister, the other blocks on PK insert until first commits → DuplicateKeyException → finds SINGLE + receipt with same amount → replay return. The loser did NOT mutate. 

Two concurrent single receipts DIFFERENT serials, same detail, same version: both register their serials, one tryAddReceived wins, other gets 0 → 409 version conflict → its serial registration rolls back. Good — serial not consumed by failed attempt.

Concurrent single vs master same serial: one wins tryRegister; loser sees kind and gets 409 (master loser: replayMaster → fingerprint mismatch → 409, or if same content... can't be same content since kinds differ — a master request and single request with same serial: single loser sees MASTER → 409 "已用于汇总分配母流水"; master loser sees SINGLE → 409 "已用于单笔追缴回执"). 

Now — the master allocate flow: tryRegister happens BEFORE tryAddReceived loop there too. In my draft above, yes: register after validations, before mutations. If tryAddReceived fails mid-loop → throw → whole tx (incl. serial registration + master row + earlier allocations) rolls back. "全笔拒绝" ✓.

Edge: replayMaster when bank_serial says MASTER but master row missing — impossible (same tx). But defensive orElseThrow ConflictException is fine.

